  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Periods of rain today through Wednesday. \nGreatest areal coverage tonight.\n\nWhat remains of an MCV that dropped 3-5 inches of rain in OH/WV\nwill move into the Laurel Highlands this morning and spread\nnorthward towards I-80 through the day. Given the lack of \ninstability in central PA, rainfall rates will be on the light \nside, generally up to 0.25 inch/hr. We expect an uptick in areal\ncoverage of the rainfall tonight as the pressure gradient\nbetween a subtle low pressure system moving into Ohio and a\nbuilding high to our north results in increasing upslope SE \nflow at 850 mb. This will be coupled with isentropic ascent and\nsome frontogenesis in the 800-600 mb layer. Even during max \nareal coverage, rain may struggle to get to the PA-NY border due\nto a sharp cutoff in moisture (PW values near 1.50\" along I-80 \nvs 0.60\" at the PA-NY border at midnight tonight). Periods of \nrain will continue through much of Tuesday before the dry air to\nour north surges southwestward late Tuesday night into \nWednesday. Highs on Tuesday will be in the 50s to low 60s, \nperhaps the coolest afternoon since late May. Lingering showers \non Wed will likely be confined to the Laurels. Highest forecast \nQPF in the Monday- Wednesday time frame is 1.00-1.50 inch in the\nLaurel Highlands, with locally higher amounts of 2.00 inches \npossible if some elevated instability moves into the region. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Trending drier later in the week as a coastal \nstorm develops offshore.\n\nNortheast flow Wednesday into Thursday between 1038 mb high in\nQuebec and a developing coastal low will advect in dewpoints in\nthe 40s and perhaps even the 30s at the sfc Wed-Thu, with even\ndrier air in the 500-850 mb layer. This should bring an end to\nthe rainfall for at least a couple of days. The next feature to\nmonitor will be the coastal storm, which may meander off the New\nEngland and Mid Atlantic coast into the early part of next week\nwith this blocking high to its north. Such blocky patterns \nare notoriously difficult to predict with respect to sfc\nfeatures and precip potential/placement/timing. Depending on the\nsize of the coastal storm and how much it retrogrades, rain will\nbe possible in at least east central PA between Fri and early\nnext week, but confidence is low. Higher confidence in wind \ngusts of 15-25 mph and below normal temps through the end of the\nweek.",
